  "memories" : [
    {
      "content" : "# Tidy Laptop\n\n**Type:** tracker. A recoverable desktop-cleanup loop: scan approved\nfolders, triage findings on a kanban, apply each fix so it can be undone.\n\n## Requires shell access\n\nEvery scan and every fix runs shell commands (`du`, `find`, `mdfind`,\n`osascript`, `mv`, `chmod`). Shell access is not granted by default, and\nthe user is asked before each command runs.\n\nCheck shell access is available before the first scan. If it is not, say\nso plainly and stop — never invent findings or guess what is on disk.\n\nThe commands assume a **macOS** host: `osascript` driving Finder is what\nmakes a delete land in the Trash with \"Put Back\" working. They run\nwherever the shell runs, which is not always the machine in front of the\nuser — somewhere without Finder the recoverable-delete guarantee does not\nhold, so stop and tell the user rather than deleting.\n\n## Components (canvas)\n\n- **Cleanup Jobs** (`tracker-1`) — one card per finding. Fields: Title,\n  Topic (Junk\/Delete, Reorganize, Security, Disk hogs), Category\n  (Suggested, Apply, In-Review, Done, Rejected), Path, Detail, LogRef.\n\n## How to use (the loop)\n\n1. `\/setup` — confirm scan scope and topics, optionally schedule scans.\n   Run this first; nothing scans until `setupComplete` is true.\n2. `\/tidy-scan` — read-only sweep of `scanScope.allow`. Adds `Suggested`\n   cards. Never modifies a file.\n3. The user drags a card to **Apply**. The `apply-cleanup` automation\n   proposes a chat (Start\/Dismiss) that runs the skill for that Topic.\n4. The fix lands recoverably, writes `tidy-log\/<itemId>.md`, sets LogRef,\n   and moves the card to **In-Review**.\n5. The user checks the result and moves the card to **Done** (or back to\n   Suggested). Only the user closes a card — never set Done yourself.\n6. `\/clean-tracker` — purge Done and Rejected. Change logs are kept.\n\n## Rules that always apply\n\n- **Never `rm`.** Deletes go to the Trash; moves are logged and\n  reversible. If you cannot do it recoverably, stop and ask.\n- **Stay inside `scanScope.allow`** (`pupa\/tidy-config.json`). Anything\n  outside it — system dirs, other users, cloud-synced folders — is out of\n  bounds until the user adds it via `\/setup`.\n- **One item per run.** A cleanup skill touches only its own card's Path.\n- **Scanning is read-only.** `\/tidy-scan` never changes a file.\n- **Secrets stay put.** When a Security scan finds a credential, record\n  where it is and what kind — never copy the secret value into a card, a\n  log, or the chat. Advise rotation; never destroy the file.\n\n## Keeping yourself updated\n\nKeep `MEMORY.md` (what this machine looks like — the folders that fill up,\nthe paths the user always keeps) and `USER.md` (their preferences: what\ncounts as junk, what must never be touched) at the memory root. Keep both\ncompact, a few hundred words each, pruning rather than appending. When the\nuser rejects a suggestion, write down why so the next scan stops proposing\nit. Confirm before overwriting either file.\n",
      "path" : "pupa\/AGENTS.md"
    },
    {
      "content" : "{\n  \"automations\": {\n    \"item.moved\": [\n      {\n        \"id\": \"apply-cleanup\",\n        \"matcher\": { \"field\": \"Category\", \"toColumn\": \"Apply\" },\n        \"action\": {\n          \"startThread\": {\n            \"prompt\": \"Tidy item \\\"{{item.title}}\\\" set to Apply. Topic: {{item.Topic}}. Path: {{item.Path}}. Fetch and follow the matching cleanup skill for this topic (Junk\/Delete -> clean-junk, Reorganize -> clean-reorganize, Security -> clean-security, Disk hogs -> clean-disk-hogs). Perform it RECOVERABLY (Trash, never rm), write a change log to memory tidy-log\/<itemId>.md, set the item's LogRef, then set Category to In-Review. Stay within approved scan scope.\"\n          }\n        },\n        \"confirm\": true\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n}\n",
      "path" : "pupa\/automations.json"
    },
    {
      "content" : "---\ndescription: Skills, subagents and multi-agent slack rooms\nwhen_to_use: when creating skills or subagents, using \/commands, or working with slack rooms\nversion: 20\n---\n- **Skills** — reusable playbooks that double as `\/commands` in chat.\n  Each app has its own set (this guide is one). Ask the agent to create\n  one, or write it yourself in the memory tree under `pupa\/skills\/`.\n- **Subagents** — named delegates with their own persona and\n  instructions. The main agent creates them and hands off focused tasks.\n- **Orchestrator** — the top-level agent: creates and manages myapps and\n  delegates work to each app's agent.\n\n**Slack rooms.** A component with channels, group DMs and DMs. The\nworkspace roster is simply *all* the myapp's subagents — creating a\nsubagent adds it to the roster; nothing lives in the component but rooms\nand their transcripts. @-mention an agent (or post in its DM) to wake it:\nit reads the channel's history for context, works with the app's tools,\nand posts its reply back to the channel. Agents can @-mention each other,\nso one message can start a chain of replies. Exporting the app keeps the\nchannels and the agent personas but strips the transcripts.",
      "path" : "pupa\/plugins\/pupa-guide\/skills\/pupa-agents\/SKILL.md"
    },
    {
      "content" : "---\ndescription: React to canvas events (an item moved) by proposing a chat — the pupa\/automations.json rule format and its guards\nwhen_to_use: when the user wants the app to act automatically on a canvas change, or when authoring or editing automation rules\nversion: 20\n---\nAn **automation** reacts *inside* a myapp to a canvas event by proposing a\nchat. Declarative config, no code — rules ride the `.pupa` bundle and only\never start a model turn, always behind a confirm bubble unless a rule\nexplicitly opts out.\n\n**Trigger (v1).** `item.moved` — a user changes any **select** field on a\ntracker item: dragging a kanban lane, editing the field on the card, or\nsaving the edit sheet. Independent of the board's group-by — a rule\nwatching `Priority` fires while the board is grouped by `Status`, and in\ngrid view too. One event per changed select field. Agent moves never\ntrigger (so a reaction can't loop on itself).\n\n**Where.** One file per app: `pupa\/automations.json`. Shape mirrors Claude\nCode hook config — an `automations` map keyed by event name, each a list of\nrules — but these are Pupa domain events, not harness hooks.\n\n```json\n{\"automations\":{\"item.moved\":[\n  {\"id\":\"review-on-move\",\n   \"matcher\":{\"field\":\"Status\",\"toColumn\":\"Review\"},\n   \"action\":{\"startThread\":{\"prompt\":\"Review {{item.title}}.\"}},\n   \"confirm\":true}\n]}}\n```\n\n**Fields.**\n- `id` (required) — unique per rule; also the lock key.\n- `matcher` — field-equality predicates, **all must hold (AND)**; omit or\n  `{}` to match every move. Keys are any of the item's own fields plus the\n  transition keys `field` \/ `toColumn` \/ `fromColumn`. Add `field` whenever\n  two select fields share an option name, or the rule fires on both.\n- `action.startThread.prompt` (required) — the chat prompt. Templates:\n  `{{item.title}}`, `{{item.<field>}}`, `{{field}}`, `{{toColumn}}`,\n  `{{fromColumn}}`, substituted literally (no code).\n- `confirm` — `true` (default) proposes a Start\/Dismiss bubble; `false`\n  auto-fires with no prompt. Only ship `confirm:false` in bundles you\n  trust — it invokes the model on a plain canvas move.\n\n**Guards (automatic — you don't configure them).**\n- **Self-mutation** — the reaction's own edits don't re-trigger the rule.\n- **Once-per-transition** — the same move fires once within a short window.\n- **In-flight lock** — while a rule's reaction for an item is running, the\n  same item won't re-fire it (a timeout backstop frees a stuck one).\n\n**Limits.** v1 has one event (`item.moved`), one action (`startThread`),\nequality-only matching, and no chaining. A malformed rule is skipped, not\nfatal — the rest of the file still loads.",
      "path" : "pupa\/plugins\/pupa-guide\/skills\/pupa-automations\/SKILL.md"
    },
    {
      "content" : "---\ndescription: The component shapes a myapp canvas can hold and how they combine\nwhen_to_use: when choosing, combining or linking components, or asked what shapes exist\nversion: 20\n---\nA myapp's canvas holds components. Available shapes:\n\n- **calculator** — live numeric model with tunable inputs + formula rows\n- **calendar** — time-indexed events in a list or month grid\n- **chart** — pie\/bar\/line visualisation of tracker or calculator data\n- **checklist** — simple done\/not-done items, no per-row fields\n- **slack** — multi-agent chat rooms (subagent personas, channels, @-mentions)\n- **tracker** — multi-field records in a table\/kanban (fields, filters, cards)\n\n**Combining.** One myapp mixes shapes freely. Items link across\ncomponents — a tracker row to a calendar event, a task to a parent\nrecord. Calculators and charts read live from other components, and a\nchart can be embedded straight into chat.\n\n**Views, not shapes.** Kanban is a tracker view mode (table ⇄ cards),\nnot a separate component.\n\n**Finding and scanning.** Both tracker views have a search box above\nthe cards — it matches any field, including link URLs, and in kanban it\nnarrows the cards without dropping columns. The shrink button next to\nthe view toggle collapses every card to its title for a whole-board\nview; it sticks per component. Cards with several links show the first\nfew and a \"+N\" you can tap to see the rest.",
      "path" : "pupa\/plugins\/pupa-guide\/skills\/pupa-components\/SKILL.md"
    },
    {
      "content" : "---\ndescription: How Pupa remembers: memories, sessions, change history, archive\nwhen_to_use: when asked what persists, how to undo changes, or how to hide an app\nversion: 20\n---\n- **Memories** — a filesystem-like tree of notes and folders that lives\n  *inside* the app, not on the device's file system. Notes are markdown\n  (`.md`); config notes can be `.json` and show verbatim as code, so\n  indentation survives. Browse and edit it from the Memories tab; the agent\n  reads and writes it too. Persists across sessions.\n- **Sessions** — \"New session\" starts a fresh conversation. The canvas\n  and memories stay. Past conversations are kept on-device and re-open with\n  their full history when you reopen the app — even after a long time away.\n- **History** — every canvas change is recorded per myapp. Browse the\n  History tab and restore any earlier state in one tap. Tap **Take\n  snapshot** to pin the current state permanently (kept forever); pinned\n  snapshots can be **Export**ed as a `.pupa` file (same share screen as\n  Settings, flagged as the pinned version). Pins survive deleting\n  the myapp — find them all in **Settings ▸ Pinned snapshots**, where a\n  deleted app can be restored (revived) from a pin.\n- **Archive** — hide a myapp without deleting it (its data and memories\n  are kept). Browse, restore or delete from Settings ▸ Archive.\n- **Recently deleted** — deleting a myapp is undoable for 180 days.\n  Settings ▸ Recently deleted lists them (including ones deleted on another\n  device, and ones a sync removed on its own) and restores the last saved\n  state — chats, components, and any skills or subagents a sync took with\n  it. Dismissing the \"Sync removed …\"\n  banner loses nothing — the app waits here. Each row can also be **deleted\n  permanently** — that erases its saved state, pinned snapshots included, on\n  every device.\n- **Recovering lost skills** — if a sync takes a skill or subagent from a\n  myapp that is still here, a \"Sync removed N memory files\" banner offers to\n  put them back. Recoverable for 30 days.",
      "path" : "pupa\/plugins\/pupa-guide\/skills\/pupa-memory\/SKILL.md"
    },
    {
      "content" : "---\ndescription: Share and install myapps as .pupa files\nwhen_to_use: when exporting, sharing, importing or installing an app\nversion: 20\n---\nA `.pupa` file is an inert snapshot of a myapp: its components, optionally\nyour records and memories, plus the app's agent instructions and skills.\nNo code runs from the file itself.\n\n**Export.** Settings ▸ Import & Export: pick components, choose whether\nrecords and memories ride along, then Share (AirDrop, Messages, Files…) —\non Mac, Save, which writes the `.pupa` wherever you choose.\nExporting a pinned snapshot (from History or Settings ▸ Pinned snapshots)\nopens this same screen with a \"Pinned version\" banner — records and memories\ndefault off there too.\n\n**Install.** Open a `.pupa` file (or import from Settings). A confirmation\nsheet shows what's inside before anything is added. Imported agent\ninstructions run with your tools once installed — only install bundles\nyou trust.\n\n**Marketplace.** Settings ▸ Import & Export ▸ Import an app links out to\npupa-app.com\/marketplace. Installing from there sends the app back to Pupa\nthrough the same confirmation sheet.",
      "path" : "pupa\/plugins\/pupa-guide\/skills\/pupa-sharing\/SKILL.md"
    },
    {
      "content" : "---\ndescription: Where things live: the on-device app vs the backend, and standing instructions\nwhen_to_use: when unsure whether something belongs in the app or the backend, which skill system you're touching, or how to change standing behaviour\nversion: 20\n---\n**App vs backend.** Everything the user sees — components and their\nitems, memories, skills, `AGENTS.md` — is app state, created and edited\n**on the user's device** through the app's own tools. The backend only\nruns the model plus a few server-side tools (web search, shell, …) and\nstores **no app data**. Never write app content to a backend path or via\na shell unless explicitly told to; if unsure where something belongs, ask.\n\n**Canvas vs memory.** Components hold data items; memory is a *separate*\nper-app markdown note tree for durable context — not canvas data. Two\ndifferent stores; don't cross them. Memory paths are an in-app structure,\nnot host file paths — never reach for it via a shell.\n\n**Two skill systems.** *App* skills are on-device `SKILL.md` files —\nyours under `pupa\/skills\/<name>\/`, bundled ones under\n`pupa\/plugins\/<plugin>\/skills\/<name>\/` (this guide is one). A backend may\nseparately expose its own read-only skills library. App skills are\nalways the on-device files.\n\n**Standing behaviour.** Persistent instructions for an app's main agent\nlive at `pupa\/AGENTS.md` (subagents: `pupa\/agents\/<name>\/AGENTS.md`).\nEdit those to change behaviour permanently — not memory, not a skill.",
      "path" : "pupa\/plugins\/pupa-guide\/skills\/pupa-system\/SKILL.md"
    },
    {
      "content" : "---\ndescription: What Pupa is and what it can do — guide entry point\nwhen_to_use: when the user asks what Pupa is, what it can do, or how its pieces fit together\nversion: 20\n---\nPupa builds apps around you: describe what you need in chat and the agent\nassembles a **myapp** — a canvas of components it edits live while you talk.\n\n**Boundaries — who is what**\n- **Orchestrator** — the top-level chat. Creates, renames, archives and\n  deletes myapps and delegates work to their agents. Has its own memories\n  and skills; no canvas of its own.\n- **Myapp** — one workspace: its own agent, canvas, memories, skills and\n  subagents.\n- **Component** — one shape on a myapp's canvas (tracker, calendar, …).\n- **Item** — one record inside a component (row, event, task…).\n- **Memories** — each myapp (and the orchestrator) keeps its own\n  filesystem-like tree of markdown notes and folders living *inside* the\n  app — not on the device's file system. Edited by you and the agent\n  alike; persists across sessions. Durable context, not records.\n\nItems can link across components *within one myapp*. Myapps don't share\ndata — an app moves between devices or people as a `.pupa` file.\n\n**Go deeper** (users: type the `\/command`; agents: `app_skill_view`)\n- \/pupa-components — the shapes a canvas can hold and how they combine\n- \/pupa-sharing — export and install myapps as `.pupa` files\n- \/pupa-memory — memories, sessions, change history, archive\n- \/pupa-agents — skills, subagents and slack rooms\n- \/pupa-automations — react to canvas events (an item moved) by proposing a chat\n- \/pupa-system — where things live: on-device app vs backend, standing instructions",
      "path" : "pupa\/plugins\/pupa-guide\/skills\/pupa\/SKILL.md"
    },
    {
      "content" : "---\ndescription: Handle a Disk hogs item — trash or archive a large file, recoverable, logged\nwhen_to_use: when a Disk hogs item is set to Apply (via automation) or \/clean-disk-hogs with an item\nuser-invocable: false\n---\n\n# clean-disk-hogs\n\nReclaim space for ONE large-file tracker item, recoverably, then log + In-Review.\n\n## Input\nThe tracker item (id, Path, Detail — includes size).\n\n## Steps\n1. Re-read item. Confirm `Topic = Disk hogs`, path inside allowed scope.\n2. Default action = move to macOS Trash (recoverable):\n   ```\n   osascript -e 'tell application \"Finder\" to delete (POSIX file \"<ABSOLUTE_PATH>\")'\n   ```\n   NEVER `rm`. If Detail says \"archive\" instead, move to the user's archive\n   folder (logged move) rather than Trash.\n3. Write change log to `tidy-log\/<itemId>.md`: timestamp, action, original path,\n   size reclaimed, Trash\/archive location, restore steps.\n4. Update item: `LogRef = tidy-log\/<itemId>.md`, `Category = In-Review`.\n5. Report space reclaimed.\n\n## Rules\n- Recoverable only. One item per run. Stay within scope.\n",
      "path" : "pupa\/skills\/clean-disk-hogs\/SKILL.md"
    },
    {
      "content" : "---\ndescription: Perform a Junk\/Delete cleanup for one tracker item — recoverable, logged\nwhen_to_use: when a Junk\/Delete item is set to Apply (via automation) or \/clean-junk with an item\nuser-invocable: false\n---\n\n# clean-junk\n\nDelete junk **recoverably** for ONE tracker item, then log and set In-Review.\n\n## Input\nThe tracker item (id, Path, Detail).\n\n## Steps\n1. Re-read the item. Confirm `Topic = Junk\/Delete` and `Path` inside allowed scope.\n2. Move the file\/folder to macOS Trash (recoverable). Use Finder so \"Put Back\"\n   works:\n   ```\n   osascript -e 'tell application \"Finder\" to delete (POSIX file \"<ABSOLUTE_PATH>\")'\n   ```\n   NEVER use `rm`. If path missing\/out of scope, stop and note it in the item.\n3. Write change log to memory root `tidy-log\/<itemId>.md`:\n   timestamp, action `trashed`, original path, Trash location, restore steps\n   (\"Finder ▸ Trash ▸ Put Back\").\n4. Update the item: `LogRef = tidy-log\/<itemId>.md`, `Category = In-Review`.\n5. Report what was trashed.\n\n## Rules\n- Recoverable only. Trash, never permanent delete.\n- One item per run. Stay within `scanScope.allow`.\n",
      "path" : "pupa\/skills\/clean-junk\/SKILL.md"
    },
    {
      "content" : "---\ndescription: Perform a Reorganize action for one tracker item — logged move, reversible\nwhen_to_use: when a Reorganize item is set to Apply (via automation) or \/clean-reorganize with an item\nuser-invocable: false\n---\n\n# clean-reorganize\n\nMove\/rename per ONE tracker item, log old→new, set In-Review.\n\n## Input\nThe tracker item (id, Path, Detail — Detail states the target location).\n\n## Steps\n1. Re-read item. Confirm `Topic = Reorganize`, source + target inside allowed scope.\n2. Create target folder if needed. Move the file\/folder:\n   `mv \"<src>\" \"<dst>\"`. Do not overwrite — if target exists, rename or stop and\n   note in the item.\n3. Write change log to `tidy-log\/<itemId>.md`: timestamp, action `moved`,\n   old path, new path, restore steps (reverse `mv`).\n4. Update item: `LogRef = tidy-log\/<itemId>.md`, `Category = In-Review`.\n5. Report old→new.\n\n## Rules\n- Reversible move only. Never delete during reorg.\n- No overwrites. One item per run. Stay within scope.\n",
      "path" : "pupa\/skills\/clean-reorganize\/SKILL.md"
    },
    {
      "content" : "---\ndescription: Perform a Security fix for one tracker item — patch\/advise, no deletion\nwhen_to_use: when a Security item is set to Apply (via automation) or \/clean-security with an item\nuser-invocable: false\n---\n\n# clean-security\n\nApply a security fix for ONE tracker item, log, set In-Review.\n\n## Input\nThe tracker item (id, Path, Detail — Detail states the issue + fix).\n\n## Steps\n1. Re-read item. Confirm `Topic = Security`.\n2. Apply the least-destructive fix for the issue type:\n   - **Outdated \/ vuln package** — upgrade to the fixed version.\n   - **World-readable key\/secret file** — tighten perms (`chmod 600`).\n   - **Exposed secret in a file\/history** — advise rotation; redact only with a\n     recoverable backup to Trash of the original. Never silently destroy data.\n   Do NOT permanently delete anything. If unsure, describe the fix and ask.\n3. Write change log to `tidy-log\/<itemId>.md`: timestamp, issue, action taken,\n   affected path, how to revert.\n4. Update item: `LogRef = tidy-log\/<itemId>.md`, `Category = In-Review`.\n5. Report the fix + any manual follow-up (e.g. rotate credential).\n\n## Rules\n- No permanent deletion. Backups go to Trash (recoverable).\n- One item per run. Stay within scope. Flag anything risky before acting.\n",
      "path" : "pupa\/skills\/clean-security\/SKILL.md"
    },
    {
      "content" : "---\ndescription: Purge finished tidy items — removes Done and Rejected from the tracker\nwhen_to_use: when the user runs \/clean-tracker or asks to clear finished\/dismissed items\n---\n\n# clean-tracker\n\nRemove completed + dismissed items from the tracker.\n\n## Steps\n1. List tracker items where `Category` is `Done` or `Rejected`.\n2. Show the count and titles. Confirm with the user before removing.\n3. Remove those items from the tracker.\n4. Keep their `tidy-log\/<itemId>.md` change logs (do not delete logs — history).\n5. Report how many were purged.\n\n## Rules\n- Only touch `Done` and `Rejected`. Never remove active items.\n- Change logs in `tidy-log\/` are kept as a record.\n",
      "path" : "pupa\/skills\/clean-tracker\/SKILL.md"
    },
    {
      "content" : "---\ndescription: Set up or reconfigure Tidy Laptop — scan scope, topics, recurring schedules, first scan\nwhen_to_use: when the user first opens Tidy Laptop, runs \/setup, or wants to change scan scope \/ topics \/ reminders\n---\n\n# setup\n\nInteractive setup. Ask ONE thing at a time, then save `pupa\/tidy-config.json`.\n\nUse the `ask_user_questions` tool for the choices below whenever it fits\n(confirming scope, picking topics, choosing cadence) — offer short options so\nthe user taps instead of typing. Fall back to plain chat only for open-ended\nanswers (e.g. custom paths).\n\n## Steps\n1. **Read** current `pupa\/tidy-config.json`. Show current scope + schedules if any.\n2. **Scan scope (privacy).** Show the default `allow` list. Ask user to confirm,\n   add, or remove paths. Never include system dirs, other users, or cloud-synced\n   dirs unless the user explicitly says so. Save `scanScope.allow`.\n3. **Topics.** Confirm which of `Junk\/Delete`, `Reorganize`, `Security`,\n   `Disk hogs` to keep active. Save `topics`.\n4. **Recurring reminders.** Ask if wanted. If yes, collect one or more\n   schedules: each is `{id, topics:[...], cadence, label}` where cadence is\n   `{kind:\"daily\", hour, minute}`, `{kind:\"weekly\", weekday, hour, minute}`\n   (weekday 1=Sunday) or `{kind:\"everyNHours\", hours}` (1-24). Those are the\n   only repeating triggers — there is no monthly option, so offer weekly.\n   Example: \"delete unused — weekly, Sunday 09:00\". Save to `schedules`,\n   then run `tidy-notify` to register the banners.\n5. Set `setupComplete: true`, write config.\n6. **Offer first scan** now: if yes, invoke `tidy-scan` for the active topics.\n\n## Rules\n- Privacy is paramount. Confirm scope before any scan. Act only within `allow`.\n- Do not scan or delete anything during setup itself — only configure.\n- Scans need shell access (see `pupa\/AGENTS.md`). Confirm it is available\n  before offering the first scan; if it is off, finish setup and say so.\n",
      "path" : "pupa\/skills\/setup\/SKILL.md"
    },
    {
      "content" : "---\ndescription: Schedule or cancel recurring tidy-scan reminders as local notification banners\nwhen_to_use: when \/setup collects schedules, or the user asks to add, change or stop recurring scan reminders\n---\n\n# tidy-notify\n\nTurn the `schedules` in `pupa\/tidy-config.json` into recurring notification\nbanners that re-run a scan when tapped.\n\n## Steps\n1. Call `get_tools_notifications` once to unlock the notification tools.\n2. Read `schedules` from `pupa\/tidy-config.json`. For each entry with no\n   live `notificationId`, call `sendNotification`:\n   - `title` — the schedule's `label`.\n   - `body` — the topics it scans.\n   - `trigger` — the schedule's `cadence`: `{kind:\"daily\", hour, minute}`,\n     `{kind:\"weekly\", weekday, hour, minute}` (weekday 1=Sunday) or\n     `{kind:\"everyNHours\", hours}` (1-24). These repeat until cancelled.\n   - `target` — `{componentId:\"tracker-1\"}`.\n   - `tapAction` — `{kind:\"runAgent\", prompt:\"\/tidy-scan <topics>\"}`, so\n     tapping the banner runs that schedule's scan.\n3. Save the returned `id` onto the schedule as `notificationId`.\n4. To drop a schedule, `cancelNotification` with its `notificationId`, then\n   remove the entry from the config.\n\n## Rules\n- Only `daily`, `weekly` and `everyNHours` repeat — there is no monthly\n  trigger. Offer weekly instead; never promise a cadence you cannot set.\n- On `notifications-not-authorized`, tell the user to enable notifications\n  and keep the schedule saved so `\/setup` can retry later.\n- A banner only ever reopens this app.\n",
      "path" : "pupa\/skills\/tidy-notify\/SKILL.md"
    },
    {
      "content" : "---\ndescription: Scan approved paths and create Suggested tidy items in the tracker\nwhen_to_use: when running a tidy scan (from setup, a reminder tap, or \/tidy-scan). Optional arg = topic(s) to scan\n---\n\n# tidy-scan\n\nScan the user's approved paths and populate the tracker with `Suggested` items.\n\n## Input\nOptional topic list (arg). If none, scan all active topics from\n`pupa\/tidy-config.json`.\n\n## Steps\n1. Read `pupa\/tidy-config.json`. Use only `scanScope.allow` paths. If\n   `setupComplete` is false, tell user to run `\/setup` first.\n2. For each active topic, scan (read-only — never modify during scan):\n   - **Junk\/Delete** — stale caches, old Downloads, duplicates, orphaned files.\n   - **Reorganize** — messy\/misplaced files, deep clutter, wrong-folder items.\n   - **Security** — exposed secrets, world-readable keys, outdated packages,\n     known-vuln deps. Read-only detection; no fixes here.\n   - **Disk hogs** — largest space consumers under allowed paths.\n   Use `du`, `find`, `mdfind`, package audit tools as needed. Stay in scope.\n3. For each finding, add a tracker item (see tracker fields): `Title`, `Topic`,\n   `Category = Suggested`, `Path`, `Detail` (why + size + proposed action).\n   Skip duplicates already in the tracker (match by Path+Topic).\n4. Report a short summary: counts per topic.\n\n## Rules\n- Read-only. This skill NEVER deletes, moves, or edits files.\n- Respect scope strictly. Skip anything outside `allow`.\n- Record where a secret lives and what kind it is — never copy its value\n  into a card, a log, or the chat.\n",
      "path" : "pupa\/skills\/tidy-scan\/SKILL.md"
    },
    {
      "content" : "---\ndescription: Record durable, app-level learnings from this conversation to pupa\/MEMORIES.md\nwhen_to_use: when the user runs \/to-memory or asks to capture what was learned this session\n---\nCapture what would help next time someone works on *this* app — especially\npoints where the user redirected you or an initial approach needed realignment.\n\n1. Re-read this conversation. Extract *general, durable* guidance: conventions\n   clarified, preferences stated, gotchas, and anything that needed\n   realignment mid-task. Skip one-off task specifics.\n2. Read the existing `pupa\/MEMORIES.md` (if present) so you merge, not duplicate.\n3. Write to `pupa\/MEMORIES.md` or your AGENTS.md file with the merged result — short bullets under clear\n   headings, the new insight integrated in place. Choose AGENTS.md for info you should always be aware of in every conversation, \n   while choose MEMORIES.md for info that is not always needed.\n4. Tell the user in one line what you added.",
      "path" : "pupa\/skills\/to-memory\/SKILL.md"
    },
    {
      "content" : "{\n  \"scanScope\": {\n    \"allow\": [\n      \"~\/Desktop\"\n    ],\n    \"allowDevCaches\": true,\n    \"deny\": [\n      \"\/System\",\n      \"\/Library\",\n      \"\/Users\/Shared\",\n      \"other users' home dirs\",\n      \"cloud-synced dirs unless explicitly allowed\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"topics\": [\n    \"Junk\/Delete\",\n    \"Reorganize\",\n    \"Security\",\n    \"Disk hogs\"\n  ],\n  \"schedules\": [],\n  \"_schedulesShape\": \"each: {id, topics:[...], label, notificationId?, cadence}; cadence is {kind:'daily',hour,minute} | {kind:'weekly',weekday,hour,minute} | {kind:'everyNHours',hours:1-24}\",\n  \"setupComplete\": false\n}",
      "path" : "pupa\/tidy-config.json"
    }
  ]
}